{0 Variables and secrets}

The [env] argument of a handler holds the configuration of the worker.
{!Workero.Env.var} reads a value by name. It returns [Some value] for a string
variable or secret, and [None] for a name that does not exist or does not hold
a string. {!Workero.Env.secret} is the same read for a value that the worker
cannot run without. It returns the string, and raises [Invalid_argument] when
the name does not exist or does not hold a string.

{@ocaml[
let handler _req env _ctx =
  match Workero.Env.var env "API_TOKEN" with
  | Some token ->
      Workero.Response.text
        (Printf.sprintf "token of %d bytes\n" (String.length token))
  | None -> Workero.Response.text ~status:`Internal_Server_Error "API_TOKEN is not set\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

A variable and a secret look the same in [env]. They differ in how you set
them. {!Workero.Env.secret} suits a secret, because a missing secret then stops
the request with a message that names it:

{@ocaml[
let handler _req env _ctx =
  let token = Workero.Env.secret env "API_TOKEN" in
  Workero.Response.text (Printf.sprintf "token of %d bytes\n" (String.length token))
]}

The message is [Workero.Env.secret: "API_TOKEN" not found], or
[Workero.Env.secret: "API_TOKEN" is an object, not a string]. It never contains
the value.

{1 Variables}

Variables are not secret. Put them in [wrangler.toml]:

{@toml[
[vars]
GREETING = "hello from vars"
]}

{1 Secrets}

Set a secret with Wrangler. It reads the value from the terminal and stores it
for the deployed worker:

{@sh[
npx wrangler secret put API_TOKEN
]}

For [wrangler dev], put local values in a [.dev.vars] file next to
[wrangler.toml], one [NAME=value] line each. Keep that file out of Git.

{1 Object variables}

A variable in [wrangler.toml] can be an object or an array:

{@toml[
[vars]
LIMITS = { max_items = 50, regions = ["eu", "us"] }
]}

{!Workero.Env.var} gives [None] for it. {!Workero.Env.json} gives it as a
[Jsonkit.t], or [None] when the name does not exist or holds a string, a number,
a boolean or [null]:

{@ocaml[
let handler _req env _ctx =
  let open Jsonkit.Of_json in
  match Workero.Env.json env "LIMITS" with
  | Some limits ->
      Workero.Response.text
        (Printf.sprintf "max %d\n" (limits |> field "max_items" int))
  | None -> Workero.Response.text ~status:`Internal_Server_Error "LIMITS is not set\n"
]}

{!Workero.JSON.of_js} and {!Workero.JSON.to_js} convert any JavaScript value
that has a JSON form. See {{!page-requests}Requests and responses} for JSON
bodies.

{1 Secrets Store}

A secret in {{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/secrets-store/}Secrets Store}
is shared between Workers. It is a binding, not a value in [env]. Declare it in
[wrangler.toml]:

{@toml[
[[secrets_store_secrets]]
binding = "API_KEY"
store_id = "<store id>"
secret_name = "api-key"
]}

{!Workero.Secret_store.of_env} opens the binding and {!Workero.Secret_store.get}
reads the value:

{@ocaml[
let handler _req env _ctx =
  let key = Workero.Secret_store.of_env env "API_KEY" in
  match Workero.Secret_store.get key with
  | Some value ->
      Workero.Response.text (Printf.sprintf "key of %d bytes\n" (String.length value))
  | None -> Workero.Response.text ~status:`Internal_Server_Error "no key\n"
]}

[get] suspends like {!Workero.Promise.await}. It gives [None] when the runtime
returns [null] or [undefined], and raises {!Workero.Js_error} when the access
fails. [of_env] raises [Invalid_argument] when [env] has no such binding.
Read the secret inside the handler. Do not log it.
